    {
      "title": "IBM using Everspin MRAM in 5th gen FlashCore Modules",
      "date": "2026-10-05",
      "source": "BlocksAndFiles",
      "url": "https://www.blocksandfiles.com/flash/2026/10/05/ibm-using-everspin-mram-in-5th-gen-flashcore-modules/5300966",
      "summary": "",
      "body": "flash\nIBM using Everspin MRAM in 5th gen FlashCore Modules\nIBM’s gen 5 FlashCore Modules use Everspin MRAM instead of capacitors to protect against data loss from power failures.\nFlashCore Modules (FCMs) are IBM’s proprietary SSDs, with the gen 5 version being computational SSDs as they feature hardware compression, deduplication, die striping, and cyber-attack detection. The FCM5 uses QLC (4 bits/cell) NAND with an SLC (1 bit/cell) incoming write zone. There are five FCM5 raw capacity points; 6.6, 12.2, 26.4, 52.8 and 105.6 TB, with the maximum effective compressed capacities being 19.8, 36.6, 79.2, 158.4 and 316.8 TB.\nMagneto-resistive RAM (MRAM) stores bits in magnetic tunnel junctions rather than as charge levels, and is non-volatile like NAND, has DRAM-class access speed (10s of nanoseconds), is byte-addressable, and has very high endurance, unlike NAND. Everspin supplies a range of MRAM chips and an Everspin MRAM presentation at the SNIA Developer Conference in San Jose in Septem",
